  // -- gathering (patch 53.417) ---------------------------------------------------
  // Chopping and mining were oscillator recipes: a triangle blip for an axe and
  // a square blip for a pick. Both fire many times per node and hundreds of
  // times an hour, which makes them among the most heard sounds in the game.
  //
  // THREE VARIANTS EACH, not one. A gathering swing repeats every second or so,
  // and one sample retriggered at that rate is the definition of cheap. The
  // runner-up takes became the variants rather than being thrown away, so the
  // variation cost nothing extra to generate. Combined with the per-play random
  // detune the same trick the anvil uses, a full tree never repeats a sound.
  //
  // MINING is deliberately BRIGHT. Metal striking stone is what "mining" sounds
  // like in a game; the picked takes sit at a spectral centroid near 6500 Hz
  // with 96 percent of their energy above 2 kHz. The dull low takes read as
  // digging, not mining, and were rejected on that number.
  // CHOPPING is the opposite: centroid near 1200 Hz with real low end, because
  // an axe into a trunk is a woody thunk with a crack on top, not a clink.
  //
  // FORAGING previously played the AXE sound, so picking a flower sounded like
  // felling a tree. It gets its own stems-and-leaves pull.
  // TIMBER is 64k rather than 96k: it is broadband crash content, the same
  // reason the campfire bed is 64k.
